      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The current trend in dancing includes \"head banging\" with extreme flexion, extension, and rotation of the head and cervical spine. We suggest that dance-related severe pain in the cervical area may result from head banging."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A cohort of 37 eighth graders ages 13 or 14 participated in a dance marathon for charity lasting 7 h. There were 26 girls and 11 boys."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "During the dance marathon, three \"heavy metal\" songs were played during which head banging could be done."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "The painful syndromes that relate to head banging were evaluated by a convenience sample of the 37 marathon dancers in the eighth grade."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "None."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "A self-selected age-matched control group is included since 17 adolescents participated in head banging and 20 did not."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the head bangers, 81.82% of the girls and 16.6% of the boys had resultant cervical spine pain that lasted 1-3 days. Only 26.2% of non-head-banging girls and 0% of non-head-banging boys had cervical spine pain lasting 1-3 days. Of all the 8th-grade participants, 62.16% had pain somewhere. Other types of pain included leg pain, back pain, and headache. Only three adolescents took any medication for their pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The head-banger's whiplash is a self-limiting painful disorder. The easy resolution of the pain problem in adolescents is a tribute to the resilience of youth."
        }
      ]},
